WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.145
- Every time John
introduces us to somebody

00:00:02.145 --> 00:00:03.798
he says we're engaged.

00:00:03.798 --> 00:00:06.381
(upbeat music)

00:00:08.426 --> 00:00:11.277
- I'm John Rael, and I
am a skeptical filmmaker.

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:12.819
- My name is Kevin Conover and

00:00:12.819 --> 00:00:14.225
I'm a Young Earth creationist.

00:00:14.225 --> 00:00:15.692
- I make films that promote science,

00:00:15.692 --> 00:00:17.606
critical thinking, skepticism.

00:00:17.606 --> 00:00:19.683
- I have a radio program where
I talk about apologetics.

00:00:19.683 --> 00:00:21.268
A lot of people don't
know what apologetics is.

00:00:21.268 --> 00:00:23.102
It comes from a Greek word, apologia,

00:00:23.102 --> 00:00:25.720
it means "to defend,"
and I teach apologetics

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.487
to Christian school students.

00:00:27.487 --> 00:00:29.910
- When it comes to
evolution versus creationism

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:34.315
I think evolution is real
and creationism is a story,

00:00:34.315 --> 00:00:35.971
it's a myth, it's a religion.

00:00:35.971 --> 00:00:37.644
- I believe in a six day creation,

00:00:37.644 --> 00:00:39.582
what it says in Genesis in the Bible.

00:00:39.582 --> 00:00:41.543
- Spending a whole day
handcuffed to anyone

00:00:41.543 --> 00:00:42.873
is gonna be awkward.

00:00:42.873 --> 00:00:44.218
- I like trying new things.

00:00:44.218 --> 00:00:45.217
I'm kind of excited.

00:00:45.217 --> 00:00:46.050
- Let's get handcuffed.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:47.051
I want to meet this guy.

00:00:47.051 --> 00:00:48.503
- I'm ready to put the handcuffs on.

00:00:48.503 --> 00:00:50.908
(upbeat music)

00:00:50.908 --> 00:00:51.785
Nice to meet you, John.

00:00:51.785 --> 00:00:54.078
- Kevin Conover? John Rael.
- John Rael.

00:00:54.078 --> 00:00:55.285
Ready to do this?

00:00:55.285 --> 00:00:56.675
- I haven't been arrested too often, so.

00:00:56.675 --> 00:00:58.837
- Oh yeah, now this will be
my first time in handcuffs.

00:00:58.837 --> 00:01:00.836
Look at that, that's the real.
- This is the real, like.

00:01:00.836 --> 00:01:01.966
- No flexibility there.

00:01:01.966 --> 00:01:05.266
We're going to be really,
really close to each other.

00:01:05.266 --> 00:01:07.521
- What are the terms of blasphemy for you?

00:01:07.521 --> 00:01:10.793
I was Catholic, so that means
I have a ton of swears in me.

00:01:10.793 --> 00:01:14.141
- You don't say things,
like "Blank damn it."

00:01:14.141 --> 00:01:17.332
And don't, say, use Jesus's name in vain.

00:01:17.332 --> 00:01:18.187
- Let's go.

00:01:18.187 --> 00:01:19.541
(laughs)

00:01:19.541 --> 00:01:20.958
Okay, here we go.

00:01:21.903 --> 00:01:24.475
- We got into the car
through the passenger side.

00:01:24.475 --> 00:01:26.006
And then crawl over to the driver seat

00:01:26.006 --> 00:01:27.139
because we're handcuffed,

00:01:27.139 --> 00:01:28.735
and I don't know how else to do this.

00:01:28.735 --> 00:01:29.863
- This guy's giving exposition.

00:01:29.863 --> 00:01:31.133
(laughter)

00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:32.825
- You're the, sorry,
I'm not so experienced

00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:34.057
with filmmaking.

00:01:34.057 --> 00:01:36.322
Okay, here we go,
hopefully we don't crash.

00:01:36.322 --> 00:01:38.030
You know, if you want to accept Christ,

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:39.157
now might be a good time.

00:01:39.157 --> 00:01:40.762
(laughs)

00:01:40.762 --> 00:01:43.388
So as soon as we got into the
car, we were talking about

00:01:43.388 --> 00:01:45.499
how do you get chlorophyll
if the sun's energy's

00:01:45.499 --> 00:01:46.775
coming to the Earth, and chlorophyll

00:01:46.775 --> 00:01:48.545
is what takes advantage
of the sun's energy.

00:01:48.545 --> 00:01:50.898
How do you get the chlorophyll to evolve

00:01:50.898 --> 00:01:52.702
if it doesn't exist
yet and there's nothing

00:01:52.702 --> 00:01:54.032
to harness the energy?

00:01:54.032 --> 00:01:55.398
- And I was like, hold on?

00:01:55.398 --> 00:01:56.778
A little biology card to play,

00:01:56.778 --> 00:01:59.861
and that's when we met Steven Orzack.

00:02:12.032 --> 00:02:14.699
- That is what I'm asking, yeah.

00:02:28.824 --> 00:02:30.000
- I was comfortable with it,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.632
I kept announcing our engagement.

00:02:31.632 --> 00:02:33.346
- And I said, "No, we're not engaged,

00:02:33.346 --> 00:02:35.099
"I have a ring, I am married."

00:02:35.099 --> 00:02:37.342
So, when did you start
getting into filmmaking?

00:02:37.342 --> 00:02:39.842
- Well, you know, you come
to LA to do the acting thing,

00:02:39.842 --> 00:02:41.757
and you're like, "Well,
I'm sick of commercials,

00:02:41.757 --> 00:02:43.994
"I'm sick of background
work, so I got to start

00:02:43.994 --> 00:02:45.452
"making my own cool stuff."

00:02:45.452 --> 00:02:46.635
And so that's kind of what I do now.

00:02:46.635 --> 00:02:47.468
What about you?

00:02:47.468 --> 00:02:49.300
Where are you taking the podcast?

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.291
- I'm sharing a Christian
perspective on the radio

00:02:51.291 --> 00:02:53.152
and that's what I've done my whole life.

00:02:53.152 --> 00:02:54.423
I've had a lot of questions about God

00:02:54.423 --> 00:02:56.106
because I kept running
into all these people

00:02:56.106 --> 00:02:57.569
that didn't believe what I believed.

00:02:57.569 --> 00:02:59.608
And so I was like,
"Man, what in the world?

00:02:59.608 --> 00:03:00.955
"What do I believe?

00:03:00.955 --> 00:03:03.299
"And how do I know that
what I believe is true?"

00:03:03.299 --> 00:03:04.818
At what point were you like,

00:03:04.818 --> 00:03:06.332
"I'm definitely an Atheist?"

00:03:06.332 --> 00:03:08.763
- I was 14 and we asked our drama teacher,

00:03:08.763 --> 00:03:10.715
"What do you believe?"

00:03:10.715 --> 00:03:13.188
And she's like, "Oh, I don't
really want to discuss it,

00:03:13.188 --> 00:03:14.349
"but I'm an Atheist."

00:03:14.349 --> 00:03:17.219
And I just started
reading all these websites

00:03:17.219 --> 00:03:21.754
and books, and I'm like,
"Oh, crap, that's what I am."

00:03:21.754 --> 00:03:24.568
- I was born with believing parents,

00:03:24.568 --> 00:03:27.852
but biblically, the argument is that

00:03:27.852 --> 00:03:30.241
it's something you have
to decide for yourself,

00:03:30.241 --> 00:03:32.424
it's not something your
parents can decide for you.

00:03:32.424 --> 00:03:35.160
- I think if anything
could change my perspective

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.479
it would be the evidence.

00:03:37.479 --> 00:03:39.542
- I'm open to having
my perspective changed.

00:03:39.542 --> 00:03:41.683
Experience has told me that's unlikely.

00:03:41.683 --> 00:03:44.811
- And then we ended up at
the Natural History Museum.

00:03:44.811 --> 00:03:46.764
- Right when we walked in
the had a dinosaur exhibit,

00:03:46.764 --> 00:03:48.075
which I was not expecting.

00:03:48.075 --> 00:03:49.605
All throughout, it's mentioning evolution,

00:03:49.605 --> 00:03:51.402
and it's mentioning millions of years.

00:03:51.402 --> 00:03:53.634
How do you know that
this creature was from

00:03:53.634 --> 00:03:54.888
all the way back then?

00:03:54.888 --> 00:03:56.528
I'm questioning everything from this

00:03:56.528 --> 00:03:57.870
evolutionary paradigm.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:00.430
- At the Creationism
Museum, as you can expect,

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:02.661
it was a little frustrating.

00:04:02.661 --> 00:04:07.074
There were children there,
and that breaks my heart.

00:04:07.074 --> 00:04:08.570
- I think it's good that kids have

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:09.991
the opportunity to choose.

00:04:09.991 --> 00:04:12.643
- We should examine them,
we should look into them,

00:04:12.643 --> 00:04:16.181
hypothetically, in our own time,
but when it comes to, like,

00:04:16.181 --> 00:04:19.571
you know, "Hey, here's the
stuff you need to learn, kids."

00:04:19.571 --> 00:04:21.554
If I had my way, we would
be in a society where

00:04:21.554 --> 00:04:24.928
Creation Museum would
never occur to somebody.

00:04:24.928 --> 00:04:28.230
- I am radically opposed
to what he just said.

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:30.683
- We got to the radio
station, and I got my man

00:04:30.683 --> 00:04:32.020
Ross Blocher on the line.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:34.808
- Is Christianity
specifically good for culture,

00:04:34.808 --> 00:04:37.367
good for society, or is it a detriment,

00:04:37.367 --> 00:04:39.200
and a drag on society?

00:04:54.772 --> 00:04:55.911
The difficulty I have is an Atheist

00:04:55.911 --> 00:04:57.991
who tells me they have a yardstick.

00:04:57.991 --> 00:04:59.995
Because, there is no yardstick except

00:04:59.995 --> 00:05:01.912
the yardstick you make.

00:05:20.228 --> 00:05:23.888
And we also have Michael
Shermer who is a publisher

00:05:23.888 --> 00:05:25.324
of Skeptic Magazine.

00:05:25.324 --> 00:05:26.763
Michael, thank you so
much for taking the time

00:05:26.763 --> 00:05:28.642
to be with us today.

00:05:28.642 --> 00:05:30.400
The Big Bang is a poof and it happened,

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.211
I mean that's what the Big Bang is, right?

00:05:32.211 --> 00:05:34.573
All the 28 billion light years across

00:05:34.573 --> 00:05:37.087
came from absolutely
nothing, that's the claim.

00:05:37.087 --> 00:05:39.249
And it came with it without a designer,

00:05:39.249 --> 00:05:41.339
or purpose, or anything.

00:05:41.339 --> 00:05:44.513
To me, if you want to talk
about, "Let's become a skeptic."

00:05:44.513 --> 00:05:46.596
I'm like, "That's crazy."

00:05:57.221 --> 00:05:59.399
It's hard for me to
understand why you would think

00:05:59.399 --> 00:06:01.798
it makes more sense that
nothing made everything.

00:06:01.798 --> 00:06:03.906
- Nobody is saying that
nothing made everything,

00:06:03.906 --> 00:06:05.998
we're saying, "We don't know, therefore,

00:06:05.998 --> 00:06:08.440
"we don't fill in our lack of knowledge

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:10.440
"with whatever we want."

00:06:12.977 --> 00:06:16.050
(laughter)

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:17.986
- This is my wife, Marissa.

00:06:17.986 --> 00:06:18.867
- [John] How are you?

00:06:18.867 --> 00:06:19.700
- Hi.

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:21.310
- He got there, and my wife immediately

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:23.487
kicked into, you know, hospitable mode,

00:06:23.487 --> 00:06:26.722
and she's just really good that way.

00:06:26.722 --> 00:06:27.639
Yeah, they're serious.

00:06:27.639 --> 00:06:28.956
- They're the real deal.

00:06:28.956 --> 00:06:30.960
I'm not worried about the handcuffs

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:34.051
as much as I'm curious
how our bathroom breaks

00:06:34.051 --> 00:06:34.884
are gonna work.

00:06:34.884 --> 00:06:39.227
- You know, that's
gonna be a little weird.

00:06:39.227 --> 00:06:40.559
Okay, ready?

00:06:40.559 --> 00:06:42.364
I'm going to the bathroom.

00:06:42.364 --> 00:06:43.759
- Oh, it's real.

00:06:43.759 --> 00:06:45.283
- [Kevin] (laughs)

00:06:45.283 --> 00:06:47.361
John, this is Emma, my daughter.

00:06:47.361 --> 00:06:49.092
- Emma, how's it going, I'm John.

00:06:49.092 --> 00:06:50.961
No pressure, I didn't mean to scare you.

00:06:50.961 --> 00:06:52.323
- Can you say nice to meet you?

00:06:52.323 --> 00:06:53.156
- Nice to meet you.

00:06:53.156 --> 00:06:53.989
- Nice to meet you.

00:06:53.989 --> 00:06:56.041
- If you guys don't want
to be on this, though.

00:06:56.041 --> 00:06:58.291
(laughter)

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:02.560
- [Kevin] You want to be on BuzzFeed?

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:03.393
(laughs)

00:07:03.393 --> 00:07:05.080
I believe murder is always wrong.

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:06.665
It emanates from God's character.

00:07:06.665 --> 00:07:07.773
So, that being the case,

00:07:07.773 --> 00:07:09.475
there's a grounding there for morality.

00:07:09.475 --> 00:07:11.175
From an Atheist perspective, you may say,

00:07:11.175 --> 00:07:12.294
"Murder is wrong."

00:07:12.294 --> 00:07:13.679
If I wasn't a Christian, I might say,

00:07:13.679 --> 00:07:15.806
"I think it's okay to
murder certain people."

00:07:15.806 --> 00:07:17.169
Well, where do you go with that?

00:07:17.169 --> 00:07:18.702
There's no grounding for morality.

00:07:18.702 --> 00:07:20.300
It'd just be your opinion
versus my opinion.

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:23.130
- Taking a life that
is unnecessarily taken

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:24.320
is not okay.

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:25.859
- Yeah, but who are you to say that?

00:07:25.859 --> 00:07:27.653
- I'm a person, I'm me.

00:07:27.653 --> 00:07:30.058
- Yeah, but why is your
opinion better than mine?

00:07:30.058 --> 00:07:32.268
- Because you want to kill--but
you would hypothetically

00:07:32.268 --> 00:07:35.886
be saying that it is okay to
kill somebody, and it's not.

00:07:35.886 --> 00:07:37.907
- Yeah, but who are you to
force your morals on me?

00:07:37.907 --> 00:07:40.564
- The moral actually would be
- You're telling me I'm wrong.

00:07:40.564 --> 00:07:42.202
- You can't throw somebody's
life away from them.

00:07:42.202 --> 00:07:43.450
- Yeah, but you're telling me I'm wrong

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:44.586
and you're right.

00:07:44.586 --> 00:07:46.553
- In this hypothetical case, yeah.

00:07:46.553 --> 00:07:47.970
- Is it okay to tell somebody else

00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:49.460
that they're wrong and you're right?

00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:51.130
- Definitely.

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:53.197
We're getting ready for bed.

00:07:53.197 --> 00:07:56.401
- (laughs) How awkward is that?

00:07:56.401 --> 00:07:58.973
- I'm kind of morbidly curious

00:07:58.973 --> 00:08:01.717
about how the sleeping
arrangement is gonna happen.

00:08:01.717 --> 00:08:03.621
- I'm a little nervous about having a guy,

00:08:03.621 --> 00:08:05.155
a stranger, sleep in my house, you know,

00:08:05.155 --> 00:08:06.732
with my wife and my kids.

00:08:06.732 --> 00:08:07.794
That's a little weird.

00:08:07.794 --> 00:08:09.262
- Go ahead and use, yeah.

00:08:09.262 --> 00:08:10.512
- Yeah, okay, there we go.

00:08:10.512 --> 00:08:11.630
Okay, that's what we're doing here.

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:14.047
I put water on my toothbrush.

00:08:16.822 --> 00:08:18.471
- You've never slept
with a stranger before.

00:08:18.471 --> 00:08:20.612
- No, that's true,
especially not a stranger

00:08:20.612 --> 00:08:23.641
like a guy on the first night, you know?

00:08:23.641 --> 00:08:25.473
First time I met you, that's really weird.

00:08:25.473 --> 00:08:27.661
And it was actually really
comfortable at first,

00:08:27.661 --> 00:08:29.589
like I was lying back,
and I was just like,

00:08:29.589 --> 00:08:31.976
we had all these pillows,
we had tons of pillows,

00:08:31.976 --> 00:08:33.768
and a gigantic teddy bear, too.

00:08:33.768 --> 00:08:37.178
I was trying really
hard not to move my arm.

00:08:37.178 --> 00:08:38.468
Because I didn't want to bother him,

00:08:38.468 --> 00:08:39.678
I didn't want to wake him up,

00:08:39.678 --> 00:08:41.424
and so I started to get
stiff, and I was like,

00:08:41.424 --> 00:08:43.293
"Oh, man, I can't move."

00:08:43.293 --> 00:08:45.474
- [John] Marissa, aren't
you guys gonna say a prayer,

00:08:45.474 --> 00:08:47.381
or something?

00:08:47.381 --> 00:08:48.940
- Do you want us to say a prayer?

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:51.494
- [John] I'm not trying to
force you into being good

00:08:51.494 --> 00:08:53.751
Christians, I didn't
think that was necessary.

00:08:53.751 --> 00:08:56.373
But I thought that was
something that people did

00:08:56.373 --> 00:08:57.424
before bedtime.

00:08:57.424 --> 00:08:59.745
- Well, normally I pray for
my kids before we go to bed,

00:08:59.745 --> 00:09:01.586
but you prevented that from happening.

00:09:01.586 --> 00:09:02.419
(laughter)

00:09:02.419 --> 00:09:03.612
- Good night, BuzzFeed.

00:09:03.612 --> 00:09:04.455
- Night.

00:09:04.455 --> 00:09:07.278
- We're doing the
breakfast handcuff thing,

00:09:07.278 --> 00:09:10.343
waking up, lovely morning,
how are you missy?

00:09:10.343 --> 00:09:11.176
- (scowls)

00:09:11.176 --> 00:09:12.268
- [John] No pressure.

00:09:12.268 --> 00:09:13.101
(laughter)

00:09:13.101 --> 00:09:15.684
(upbeat music)

00:09:16.723 --> 00:09:18.491
- Overall, when I was getting involved in

00:09:18.491 --> 00:09:20.816
getting handcuffed to
somebody who completely

00:09:20.816 --> 00:09:23.006
disagrees with me, I was thinking, like,

00:09:23.006 --> 00:09:24.152
"What is the point of this?"

00:09:24.152 --> 00:09:25.449
As I thought more about it,

00:09:25.449 --> 00:09:28.192
I think for me the big takeaway is that

00:09:28.192 --> 00:09:30.774
it really helps me to
see the humanity of John.

00:09:30.774 --> 00:09:32.503
I got to know him as a person.

00:09:32.503 --> 00:09:34.050
What was cool about this whole experience

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:37.187
is it's great realizing there are people

00:09:37.187 --> 00:09:39.616
who disagree with you
about a particular subject,

00:09:39.616 --> 00:09:41.755
but that's not necessarily
what defines them.

00:09:41.755 --> 00:09:43.508
There's so much more to a person than just

00:09:43.508 --> 00:09:45.372
their opinion on creation and evolution,

00:09:45.372 --> 00:09:46.665
or whatever it may be.

00:09:46.665 --> 00:09:49.359
- Yeah, it was nice coming face-to-face

00:09:49.359 --> 00:09:51.508
with creationists, not just because,

00:09:51.508 --> 00:09:54.755
"Hey, you're a human, I'm
a human, it's all good."

00:09:54.755 --> 00:09:58.255
But to actually gain respect for who he is

00:10:01.136 --> 00:10:02.943
and the way he thinks.

00:10:02.943 --> 00:10:06.610
To know that as much
as I might feel one way

00:10:08.163 --> 00:10:12.246
about his ideology or what
he thinks or believes,

00:10:14.011 --> 00:10:16.053
I can't necessarily feel that way

00:10:16.053 --> 00:10:18.148
about him directly as a person.

00:10:18.148 --> 00:10:19.612
- I mean, the conversations we had,

00:10:19.612 --> 00:10:20.548
I mean there's no doubt about,

00:10:20.548 --> 00:10:24.710
he's a softie underneath
this cold, hard exterior.

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:25.616
- You're touching me.

00:10:25.616 --> 00:10:26.927
- (laughs)

00:10:26.927 --> 00:10:27.844
Here we go.

00:10:28.757 --> 00:10:30.674
This is the big moment.

00:10:33.074 --> 00:10:34.910
- And, there we go.

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:36.344
- Ah, yes.

00:10:36.344 --> 00:10:37.942
- Goodness.

00:10:37.942 --> 00:10:40.609
- Ah, I feel like I can stretch.

00:10:41.643 --> 00:10:43.323
Praise the Lord.

00:10:43.323 --> 00:10:45.573
(laughter)

